"reasoning": "The patient presents with high fever, severe chills (described as shivering and feeling cold), and a rash characterized by rashes on the arms and red patches on the neck. This clinical triad is highly characteristic of dengue fever. While malaria also features high fever and chills, it typically does not present with a prominent rash. Chicken pox and impetigo involve rashes but are distinguished by vesicular/blistery lesions or sores rather than flat red patches, and they do not classically present with severe shivering as a primary symptom. Pneumonia would require respiratory symptoms like coughing or shortness of breath, which are absent. Therefore, dengue is the most accurate diagnosis based on the symptom profile.",
"final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
